---
import IconifyIcon from '@/components/IconifyIcon.astro';
import { ABILITY_LABELS, ABILITY_MARKS, ABILITY_NAMES } from './dice-utils';
---

<div
  x-data="diceRoller"
  @keydown.escape.window="cancelSwap()"
  class="dice-roller-needs-js dr not-content"
>
  <!-- With scripting off, every control below is inert. A `<noscript>` block
       cannot remove them, so the note leads and hides them instead of leaving a
       live-looking button that does nothing. `is:inline` is required: Astro would
       otherwise hoist this stylesheet into the head unconditionally, where it
       would hide the controls for everyone, scripting enabled or not. -->
  <noscript>
    <style is:inline>
      .dice-roller-needs-js > .js-only {
        display: none !important;
      }
    </style>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gold-rule bg-bark-100 p-4 text-ink dark:border-gold-rule-dark dark:bg-bark-800 dark:text-parchment">
      <p class="font-medium">The Dice Roller needs JavaScript.</p>
      <p class="mt-1">Every other page on this site works without it. With it enabled you get 4d6, drop the lowest die, and add the remaining three per ability.</p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <div class="js-only dr-panel" role="group" aria-label="Dice roller">
    <!-- The head, as the character Card's header: a printed title over a meta
         line, closed by a 2px gold rule at its own lower edge. That rule is
         what makes it work - a head banded only by a dotted leader reads as
         another row of the sheet, and a head closed by a rule at its lower edge
         reads as a heading over what it heads.

         A printed title and a row label, and no heading element anywhere on the
         surface: Starlight colours every heading in the content column to the
         accent (0-1-1 light, 0-2-1 dark), which outranks a utility class, so a
         heading here would be an accent-coloured label the surface never asked
         for. The page's own `h1` is already "Dice Roller". -->
    <div class="dr-head">
      <div class="dr-head-id">
        <p class="dr-title">Dice Roller</p>
        <p class="dr-meta">4d6, drop the lowest die, add the rest.</p>
      </div>
      <!-- The one primary action.

           The label is `--sl-color-text-invert` and must not be pinned to a
           literal, because the FILL is a different colour in each theme and the
           one legible ink is not the same in both:

             light  fill #3f4a3a (dark sage)  text-invert  9.33:1
             dark   fill #f7860f (Sap Amber) text-invert  5.28:1

           Bark Black on the same two fills is 7.09:1 in dark and 1.91:1 in
           light, so a single literal is a fail in one theme or the other. The
           fill is --sl-color-accent-contrast and never the plain accent, since
           the plain accent under inverted text is the pairing that fails.

           Measure this one settled. The button transitions
           `background-color` over 150ms, so reading `getComputedStyle` on the
           frame after a theme switch returns an interpolated colour: sampled
           that way it reports 2.51:1, which is a light-theme label on a
           dark-theme fill - a pairing the page never renders. The gilt inner
           rule below is the refinement. -->
      <button
        type="button"
        @click="rollAll()"
        :disabled="isRolling"
        :class="{ 'is-rolling': isRolling }"
        class="dr-roll bg-(--sl-color-accent-contrast) text-(color:--sl-color-text-invert) [@media(hover:hover)]:hover:bg-(--sl-color-accent-high)"
        aria-label="Roll all ability scores"
      >
        <IconifyIcon icon="game-icons:dice-twenty-faces-twenty" width="1.125rem" class="dr-mark" />
        <span x-text="isRolling ? 'Rolling...' : 'Roll All Abilities'"></span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- The six marks, drawn once and drawn by reference.
         A row below is cloned by an `x-for`, so the template cannot know which
         ability it is being cloned for: an icon rendered inline in the template
         would be the same icon six times, once per row. So each mark is emitted
         here as a `<symbol>` named for its ability, and each row draws its own
         with a `<use>` whose href is that name.

         The mark travels with the name because the href is the name and nothing
         else. A swap exchanges two rows' dice and leaves every name where it was,
         so a mark bound to the row rather than to the ability would end up beside
         the wrong name the moment a swap was confirmed.

         The names come from `ABILITY_LABELS` and the icons from `ABILITY_MARKS`,
         both of which are declared once beside the ability codes in the shared
         data module and re-exported to Point Buy rather than redeclared there,
         because both tools print the same six abilities and a second table would
         be a list to keep in step. The icons are a `game-icons` family
         throughout, so the two panels read as one set of instruments rather than
         as six borrowed glyphs beside six others. -->
    <svg class="dr-mark-sprite" aria-hidden="true">
      {
        ABILITY_NAMES.map((code) => (
          <IconifyIcon icon={ABILITY_MARKS[code]} symbolId={`dr-mark-${ABILITY_LABELS[code]}`} />
        ))
      }
    </svg>

    <div class="js-only">
      <template x-for="(ability, index) in abilities" :key="ability.name">
        <!-- `relative` is load-bearing, not decoration. The swap panel is out
             of flow below its own row and lifted by z-index, so a staged swap
             can never be covered by a later row's tiles - which is exactly what
             happened when the panel was in flow, and why the tool's only
             destructive action could be neither committed nor dismissed with a
             pointer. -->
        <div
          class="dr-row"
          :class="{ 'is-swapped': stagedSwap && (stagedSwap.index1 === index || stagedSwap.index2 === index) }"
        >
          <div class="dr-id">
            <!-- The trade handle. Two presses exchange two abilities' rolls.
                 It is a button and not the whole row because the row already
                 holds a 44px re-roll and a dice cluster, and a row-sized target
                 containing them is a mis-tap waiting to happen. It borrows no
                 border or fill from the re-roll beside it, so the name still
                 reads as writing, and it takes only the 44px floor the row
                 already has. `aria-pressed` carries the state, never a class
                 alone. -->
            <button
              type="button"
              @click="selectAbility(index)"
              :aria-pressed="selectedIndex === index"
              :aria-label="'Select ' + ability.name + ' for swap'"
              class="dr-trade"
            >
              <!-- The name, with its mark. One flex line rather than a name with a
                   mark above it: the mark identifies the ability and the name says
                   which, so they are read as one object, and six rows of two lines
                   would read as twelve rows. `dr-mark-line` is baseline-aligned so
                   a 1.125rem silhouette sits on the name's own baseline instead of
                   floating above it, and the mark is `aria-hidden` because it is a
                   picture of what the name already spells out - the button's own
                   label carries the ability and the action, and is unchanged. -->
              <span class="dr-mark-line">
                <svg class="dr-ability-mark" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <use :href="'#dr-mark-' + ability.name"></use>
                </svg>
                <span class="dr-name" x-text="ability.name"></span>
              </span>
              <!-- The arithmetic. It is the whole record of which three dice
                   counted, and it is here rather than in the score plate
                   because the plate is one figure and this is a sentence about
                   the row. It also carries the arrival state: with no roll yet
                   this line is the prompt, so the row is never blank. -->
              <span
                class="dr-calc"
                x-text="ability.dice.length ? ability.topThree.join(' + ') : 'Roll to generate'"
              ></span>
            </button>

            <!-- The ledger leader. It exists because of the width, not as
                 ornament: the content column runs to about 880px and the row
                 holds four numbers, so with the name left-packed and the tray
                 hard against the score there was roughly 600px of bare panel
                 between a row's subject and its own evidence. The dotted rule is
                 Point Buy's, and it is what turns that span back into one written
                 line. Decorative, so it is hidden from the tree rather than
                 read as an empty node. -->
            <span class="dr-leader" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <!-- The tray. Four dice in a recessed well, and it is never empty:
                 an unrolled ability renders `emptyFaces` (face 0, no pips), so a
                 row arrives complete - you see the 4d6 waiting rather than a
                 gap - and the tray's width never changes, so the six scores
                 stay on one column and the sheet never reflows under a roll.
                 The socket is `aria-hidden`: the row's own arithmetic line
                 already says "Roll to generate", and six rows of four
                 unrolled dice would otherwise be twenty-four announcements of
                 nothing. -->
            <div class="dr-dice">
              <template x-for="(die, i) in ability.dice.length ? ability.dice : emptyFaces" :key="i">
                <span
                  class="dr-die"
                  :data-face="die"
                  :class="ability.dice.length ? (ability.topThreeIndices.includes(i) ? 'is-kept' : 'is-dropped') : 'is-socket'"
                  role="img"
                  :aria-hidden="!ability.dice.length"
                  :aria-label="'Die ' + die + (ability.topThreeIndices.includes(i) ? '' : ', dropped')"
                >
                  <template x-for="p in 9"><i class="dr-pip"></i></template>
                </span>
              </template>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- The score plate. This is the surface's one piece of hierarchy:
               the rolled score is the number a player came for, and the dice
               beside it are the evidence for it rather than four competing
               figures. `is-empty` is the arrival state - a scored nothing, in
               the soft ink, so six unrolled plates do not read as six zeros. -->
          <div class="dr-plate" :class="ability.dice.length ? '' : 'is-empty'">
            <span
              class="dr-sum"
              :class="ability.dice.length ? '' : 'is-empty'"
              x-text="ability.dice.length ? ability.sum : '·'"
            ></span>
            <span
              class="dr-mod"
              x-text="ability.dice.length ? formatModifier(ability.modifier) : ''"
            ></span>
          </div>

          <!-- The re-roll. The target is the control rather than the glyph
               inside it (WCAG 2.5.8), the ring is never the colour of the glyph
               it outlines, and the hover tint sits inside `@media (hover: hover)`
               because `:hover` sticks after a tap on iOS. Press feedback is
               deliberately ungated, for the reason the row gives. -->
          <button
            type="button"
            @click="rollIndividual(index)"
            :disabled="isRolling || ability.rolling || selectedIndex !== null"
            class="dr-reroll"
            :aria-label="'Re-roll ' + ability.name"
          >
            <IconifyIcon icon="mdi:refresh" width="1rem" class="dr-glyph" />
          </button>

          <div x-show="ability.rolling" class="dr-pulse"></div>

          <template x-if="stagedSwap && stagedSwap.index1 === index">
            <div class="dr-swap">
              <div
                class="dr-swap-name"
                x-text="abilities[stagedSwap.index1].name + ' ↔ ' + abilities[stagedSwap.index2].name"
              ></div>
              <div class="flex justify-center gap-2 not-content">
                <button
                  type="button"
                  @click="confirmSwap()"
                  class="dice-round-btn dice-round-btn--confirm"
                  aria-label="Confirm swap"
                >
                  <IconifyIcon icon="mdi:check" width="1em" />
                </button>
                <button
                  type="button"
                  @click="cancelSwap()"
                  class="dice-round-btn dice-round-btn--cancel"
                  aria-label="Cancel swap"
                >
                  <IconifyIcon icon="mdi:close" width="1em" />
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
          </template>
        </div>
      </template>
    </div>

    <!-- The sheet's own strength, as one figure. It is the question a player asks
         of a roll - "is this a good sheet" - and until now the panel answered it
         only by making the reader add six numbers up themselves, which is the one
         piece of arithmetic a sheet should never ask for.

         In the foot rather than the head, as the session figures below it are: a
         governing number set in a title band reads as a headline instead of as the
         state of the sheet. And above the Stats block rather than inside it: that
         block is a window over the whole session and does not exist until a full
         roll has happened, while this is a statement about the sheet on screen
         and is true after a single roll. Gating it with the Stats block would
         hide the one figure that is always answerable.

         The label says which sheet it means. The number is identical either way -
         a session's scores sum to a sheet's score - so the scope can only be
         stated in words, and without it this reads as another sample beside one
         that is explicitly a 50-roll sample.

         A plain `0` before anything is rolled, the way a blank Point Buy sheet
         states its own starting total: zero is a true statement about an unrolled
         sheet rather than a result pretending to be one, and it is plain because
         `+0` is not a thing a modifier does. -->
    <div class="dr-foot">
      <p class="dr-total-line">
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Modifier total</span>
        <span class="dr-total" x-text="modifierTotal()">0</span>
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label dr-total-scope">this sheet</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The session figures, in the foot rather than in the head: a governing
         number set in a title band reads as a headline instead of as the state
         of the sheet. The sample is capped, so the band says which window it
         is - a table comparing tonight's average against an earlier one needs to
         know it is a window, and how wide. -->
    <div class="dr-foot">
      <div class="dr-foot-head">
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Stats</span>
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label">last 50 rolls</span>
      </div>
      <template x-if="!stats || !stats.average || sessionRolls.length === 0">
        <div class="dr-void">
          <IconifyIcon icon="game-icons:dice-twenty-faces-twenty" width="1.5rem" />
          <p>No stats yet! Roll all abilities to see your results.</p>
        </div>
      </template>
      <template x-if="stats && stats.average && sessionRolls.length > 0">
        <div class="dr-stats">
          <div class="dr-stat">
            <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Average</span>
            <span class="dr-stat-fig" x-text="stats?.average"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="dr-stat">
            <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Median</span>
            <span class="dr-stat-fig" x-text="stats?.median"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="dr-stat">
            <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Lowest</span>
            <span
              class="dr-stat-fig"
              x-text="stats?.lowest + (stats?.lowestCount > 1 ? ' (x' + stats?.lowestCount + ')' : '')"
            ></span>
          </div>
          <div class="dr-stat">
            <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Highest</span>
            <span
              class="dr-stat-fig"
              x-text="stats?.highest + (stats?.highestCount > 1 ? ' (x' + stats?.highestCount + ')' : '')"
            ></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </template>
    </div>

    <!-- The log. Its heading is `x-cloak`-governed by the same `x-show` as the
         list, so one condition owns one region. `unshift` puts the newest
         first, so index 0 is the roll you just watched happen and it takes the
         action accent; older lines recede one step. -->
    <div class="dr-foot" x-cloak x-show="resultLog.length > 0">
      <div class="dr-foot-head">
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label">Roll log</span>
        <span class="dr-soft micro-label">newest first</span>
      </div>
      <div class="dr-log">
        <template x-for="(entry, i) in resultLog" :key="i">
          <div
            class="dr-log-line"
            :class="i === 0 ? 'is-current' : ''"
            x-text="entry"
          ></div>
        </template>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite" x-text="announcement"></p>
</div>

<style>
  /* One system inverted, not a second design - the same move the Ledger Panel
     makes and for the same reason. The surface steps down, the ink steps up,
     the rule swaps to its dark step, and the accent swaps from Oxblood to Gold
     Leaf. Nothing changes shape, size or spacing between themes.

     The tool surface is a themed surface. An earlier build kept its paper the
     same parchment in both themes on the reasoning that a physical object
     should not invert; the effect was that the panel and its heading were
     identical in light and dark, and the object outweighed the prose around
     it. One bark step plus the gold rule per theme, and no private material
     vocabulary. */
  .dr {
    --dr-surface: var(--color-bark-100);
    --dr-raised: var(--color-bark-200);
    /* A themed well, which this was not. `--dr-well` was bark-black in both
       themes, so the roll log rendered dark-on-dark in light: 2.47:1 for an
       ordinary line and 3.48:1 for the current one, against a 4.5:1 bar, and a
       black slab in the middle of a parchment panel - the one surface on the
       tool that refused to invert, which is the failure this panel's own
       comment above spends a paragraph ruling out. It is the same recessed step
       as the dice tray, so a panel now has exactly one well value in it. */
    --dr-well: var(--color-bark-200);
    --dr-rule: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-gold-rule) 42%, transparent);
    --dr-rule-strong: var(--color-gold-rule);
    --dr-ink: var(--color-ink);
    --dr-ink-soft: var(--color-bark-600);
    --dr-heading: var(--color-oxblood);
    --dr-accent: var(--sl-color-accent);
    /* The focus ring is never the value of the glyph it outlines. */
    --dr-focus: var(--color-bark-black);
    /* The tray the four dice sit in, and the die's own three paints. The tray is
       one ramp step *into* the panel in each theme - bark-200 under bark-100 in
       light, bark-black under bark-800 in dark - because a recess goes away from
       the light in both, and a tray that stayed one fixed value across themes
       would be the object that refused to invert. The dice are a themed surface
       too: holding them to a single bone in both themes made them the loudest
       thing on the panel in dark, which is the one outcome DESIGN.md's Don'ts
       already rejected twice on this tool. */
    --dr-tray: var(--color-bark-200);
    --dr-die-face: var(--color-bark-100);
    --dr-die-edge: var(--color-bark-600);
    --dr-die-pip: var(--color-bark-black);
    --dr-die-size: 1.75rem;
    /* The mark beside each name, and the gap between them. A token rather than a
       literal on both sides, because the handle's floor is computed from them:
       a mark widened without the floor following would push the longest name off
       a 320px row while every other row stayed inside its number. */
    --dr-mark-size: 1.125rem;
    --dr-mark-gap: 0.375rem;
    /* The floor the ability names themselves need: "Constitution" is the longest
       of the six, at about 6.4rem in Cinzel at 1rem, so this is that measure with
       a little slack rather than a second guess at it. */
    --dr-name-floor: 6.5rem;
  }

  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .dr {
    --dr-surface: var(--color-bark-800);
    --dr-raised: var(--color-bark-700);
    --dr-well: var(--color-bark-black);
    --dr-rule: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-gold-rule-dark) 45%, transparent);
    --dr-rule-strong: var(--color-gold-rule-dark);
    --dr-ink: var(--color-parchment);
    --dr-ink-soft: var(--color-bark-400);
    --dr-heading: var(--color-gold);
    --dr-focus: var(--color-bark-100);
    --dr-tray: var(--color-bark-black);
    --dr-die-face: var(--color-bark-700);
    /* Gold Rule Dark, not bark-600, and this is measured rather than chosen by
       eye. The die's 1px rim is the only thing separating a bone die from the
       bark-black tray it sits in - the fills are one ramp step apart and measure
       1.51:1 - and bark-600 on bark-black is 2.47:1, under the 3:1 a graphic
       needs to be understood. Gold Rule Dark reaches 3.86:1 on the same tray,
       which is also why a gilt-rimmed die is the right object here. In light the
       ramp's own bark-600 is already 6.15:1 against the bark-200 tray, and the
       gold rule there would drop to 1.95:1, so the edge is a per-theme token
       rather than one value held across both. */
    --dr-die-edge: var(--color-gold-rule-dark);
    --dr-die-pip: var(--color-parchment);
  }

  /* Preflight is deliberately off in this project (src/styles/tailwind.css), so
     nothing declares border-box globally. Every sized box on this surface says
     so here rather than each one carrying the declaration. */
  .dr,
  .dr *,
  .dr *::before,
  .dr *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .dr-panel {
    position: relative;
    padding: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-rule-strong);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--dr-surface);
    box-shadow: 0 1px 2px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 0.05);
  }

  /* The cap, drawn over the border so the 1px edge stays continuous. Oxblood in
     light, Gold Leaf in dark: the one place on the surface where the theme
     shows through as colour, and the only accent bar on it. */
  .dr-panel::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: -1px;
    top: -1px;
    height: 3px;
    border-radius: 8px 8px 0 0;
    background: var(--dr-heading);
  }

  /* No shadow in dark. DESIGN.md's card-rest is 5% black, which cannot lift a
     surface off bark-black, so the dark panel carries none and lets the 1px
     border, the 3px cap and the head's 2px rule do the structural work. */
  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .dr-panel {
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  .dr-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.625rem 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--dr-rule-strong);
  }

  .dr-head-id {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* A printed title, at the Card Heading's name step. Never a heading element -
     see the note in the markup. */
  .dr-title {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-heading);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--dr-heading);
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }

  /* The meta line: the Card header's meta, uppercased and tracked, so the head
     is recognisably the same object as the one on a character Card. It is the
     one place a full sentence is set this way, and it works because the
     sentence is a label - what the panel is - and not prose anyone reads at
     length. */
  .dr-meta {
    margin-top: 3px;
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
  }

  /* The one primary action on the surface. The fill and the label colour are
     utilities on the element, not declarations here: the pair is the one #329
     settled and `src/styles/tailwind.test.ts` guards at the markup, and the
     hover is gated on `@media (hover: hover)` for the reason ADR-0009 gives.
     44px is the same floor, and the box is 44 rather than the glyph's 18, so
     the target is the control rather than the mark inside it. */
  .dr-roll {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transition: background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  /* The gilt inner rule: a hairline of Gold Rule set 3px inside the slab, so the
     primary action is a struck plate with an edge rather than a coloured
     rectangle. The radius is the button's own 8px less the 3px inset, so the
     two corners stay parallel. It is a gradient rather than the bare rule token
     because a 1px solid `#c9ad6a` on the accent is a 1.31:1 hairline in light
     and reads as dirt; at 40% it is a glint that survives both fills, and it is
     decoration, so it carries no contrast obligation of its own. The label and
     the mark sit above it by source order. */
  .dr-roll::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 3px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-gold-rule) 40%, transparent);
    border-radius: 5px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The authored moment. One motion on the surface, and it is on the control
     the visitor is about to press rather than scattered across the rows: the
     die turns over as the button goes down. Ungated, for the reason the roll
     button's press already gives - a gated press excludes touch. */
  .dr-mark {
    transition: transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.9, 0.25, 1);
  }

  .dr-roll:active:not(:disabled) .dr-mark {
    transform: rotate(-32deg) scale(0.88);
  }

  /* The hover turn, and the reason it is an animation rather than a transition.

     The obvious way to write this is `transition: transform` plus
     `transform: rotate(360deg)` on hover. It renders nothing: a full revolution
     lands on the angle it started from, so the transition has identical start
     and end poses and interpolates between two identical matrices - a die that
     does not move, on a control whose whole job is to answer back before the
     press. Any angle short of 360 leaves the die standing on edge when the
     pointer leaves, which is worse than no motion at all.

     So it is a one-shot keyframe animation, which can carry the die through the
     whole revolution and hand it back at the resting pose when it ends. The
     scale bump is what makes the turn legible in the frames nearest the press:
     the first thing a visitor sees is the die change size, and the revolution
     is what they then read as the cause.

     520ms is one deliberate revolution at a rate that reads as a turn rather
     than a wobble, and the ease is out-heavy so most of the travel happens
     early - a linear 360 reads as a slow grind, which is not what a die does.

     No fill mode, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. The last keyframe
     is the resting pose, so a fill has nothing to hold - and `both` actively
     costs the press feedback: a filling animation keeps applying its final
     value, and an animation outranks a normal declaration in the cascade, so a
     filled turn silently overrode `.dr-roll:active`'s tilt and left the press
     with no die feedback at all in the one case that matters, which is pressing
     with the pointer already on the control. */
  @keyframes dr-mark-turn {
    0% {
      transform: rotate(0deg) scale(1);
    }
    45% {
      transform: rotate(196deg) scale(1.14);
    }
    100% {
      transform: rotate(360deg) scale(1);
    }
  }

  @keyframes dr-mark-spin {
    from {
      transform: rotate(0deg) scale(1.06);
    }
    to {
      transform: rotate(360deg) scale(1.06);
    }
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .dr-roll:hover:not(:disabled) .dr-mark {
      animation: dr-mark-turn 520ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.75, 0.3, 1);
    }
  }

  /* The rolling loop. Bounded by the roll rather than by a timer, because the
     state that starts it is the state that ends it: `is-rolling` is set from
     `isRolling`, which the button is already disabled from and which the last
     ability's timer clears. So there is nothing to cancel and nothing that can
     outlive the roll - remove the class and the animation stops on the frame.

     Linear and deliberately without a fill mode, so it stops *dead*. No fill means
     no spin-down and no easing curve to unwind, and the problem with a spin-down
     is that a die still easing round after the score lands reads as a result that
     is not finished yet. Continuous rather than a single nudge for the same
     reason: the roll is six abilities at 450ms each, so it runs 2700ms, and a
     one-shot 520ms turn would be over inside the first fifth of it and leave a
     disabled button looking inert for the rest. At 700ms a revolution the roll
     covers about 3.9 turns - fast enough to read as a die, slow enough not to
     strobe.

     Not hover-gated, because it never was a hover: the button is disabled for
     the whole roll, so `:hover` cannot match and cannot be the signal. */
  .dr-roll.is-rolling .dr-mark {
    animation: dr-mark-spin 700ms linear infinite;
  }

  /* Both motions are decorative loops, so both are suppressed for a visitor who
     has asked for reduced motion - the same rule the panel's own rolling
     indicator above already follows, and both neutralised by name rather than by
     a blanket `animation: none` on the surface, which would also have stopped
     the indicator and left the roll with no motion at all.

     Press feedback is deliberately not in this list. It is not a decorative
     loop, and gating it on `hover` excludes touch, which is the asymmetry
     ADR-0009 decision 4 is about. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .dr-roll:hover:not(:disabled) .dr-mark,
    .dr-roll.is-rolling .dr-mark {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  .dr-roll:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--dr-focus);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* Press feedback is deliberately ungated: `hover` sticks after a tap on iOS
     and never fires for a stylus, so gating the press on `hover` silently
     excludes exactly the device ADR-0009 is about. */
  .dr-roll:active:not(:disabled) {
    transform: translateY(1px);
  }

  .dr-roll:disabled {
    opacity: 0.5;
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  .dr-row {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 5rem 2.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--dr-rule);
  }

  .dr-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* A staged swap tints the two rows it touches, so the pair is findable in a
     column of six without a second full-strength ring competing with the one
     that means something. */
  .dr-row.is-swapped {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dr-accent) 8%, transparent);
  }

  .dr-id {
    display: flex;
    min-width: 0;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.875rem;
  }

  /* The leader, out to the tray. `flex: 1` with a floor of 1rem rather than a
     fixed width, so it takes exactly the slack the content column has and
     disappears on its own at a narrow one. It is centred on the row rather than
     on the arithmetic line, which is the same baseline the dice and the score
     sit on - a leader is a rule, and a rule belongs to the row. */
  .dr-leader {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 1rem;
    align-self: center;
    border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--dr-rule-strong);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* The trade handle. `min-width` is computed rather than written, and that is
     the load-bearing part: the mark's size and the gap beside it are added to the
     floor the names need, so widening the mark moves the floor with it. Written
     out longhand it would be a number that has to be remembered, and the day the
     mark grew, the floor would not - the row would push its longest name off a
     320px viewport, and the narrowest-row assertion below would still be
     checking the number in the stylesheet rather than the number on screen.

     It is also what that assertion reads, so a floor below the real content would
     let it pass on a row wider than the number it checked. */
  .dr-trade {
    display: flex;
    min-width: calc(var(--dr-name-floor) + var(--dr-mark-size) + var(--dr-mark-gap));
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: transparent;
    text-align: start;
    color: inherit;
    transition: box-shadow 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .dr-trade:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--dr-focus);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .dr-trade:active {
    transform: translateY(1px);
  }

  /* The mark sprite. Nothing is drawn where it is declared - the rows below
     reference it by id - so it takes no space and no pointer events, and neither
     the width nor the height attributes are declared: a zero-sized box would be
     laid out and could swallow a press at the top of the panel, which is exactly
     what `display: none` avoids. A `<use>` still resolves a symbol out of a hidden
     tree - the symbol is never rendered in its own right anyway - so hiding the
     library costs the rows nothing. */
  .dr-mark-sprite {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The mark and the name on one line, which is what keeps six marked rows
     reading as six rows. Baseline rather than centre, so a silhouette sits on the
     name's own baseline; `dr-name` has no descenders to align to at 1rem in the
     heading face, and centring it lifted the mark above the cap height. */
  .dr-mark-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--dr-mark-gap);
  }

  /* The mark. A fixed box and `flex-shrink: 0` rather than a sized-by-content
     box: the longest name has to fit beside it without the mark being the thing
     that gives way, or the six rows stop lining up. Soft ink, a step down from
     the name's own - six full-strength marks beside six names would be six times
     the ink for one more reading of the same word, and the accent belongs on this
     surface only where something is selectable.

     Not `.dr-mark`, which is the die on the roll button: one rule that animated a
     transform on the roll button's glyph and sized the row's silhouette at the
     same time would be a coupling neither of the two asked for. */
  .dr-ability-mark {
    width: var(--dr-mark-size);
    height: var(--dr-mark-size);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
  }

  /* The name carries the row's identity, so it is set in the heading face at
     body scale and in the row's own ink - the accent is for the action and the
     title, and spending it on six names would leave the title with nothing. */
  .dr-name {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-heading);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--dr-ink);
  }

  .dr-calc {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The tray. A recessed well the four dice sit in, held to its own width by
     its contents so the six score plates below it stay on one column whether
     the row is rolled or not. `border-box` is inherited from the preflight block
     at the top of this stylesheet, so the padding is inside the measured tray
     rather than added to it. */
  .dr-dice {
    display: flex;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3125rem;
    padding: 0.3125rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-rule);
    border-radius: 7px;
    background: var(--dr-tray);
  }

  /* The die. Nine pip slots on a 3x3 grid, lit by the face value, so a d6 is
     drawn as a d6 rather than as its numeral - and so the dropped die still
     shows which face it was, which a numeral with a strikethrough cannot do for
     two identical faces or for anyone who cannot resolve a 1px rule at 12px.

     Boundary and fill carry "this die counted" and "this die was dropped"; the
     face itself is de-emphasised, which WCAG 1.4.3 exempts because a discarded
     die is inactive content. Face, edge and pip are three separate tokens
     because the die sits inside the tray rather than directly on the panel, so
     none of them can be the panel's own surface any more. */
  .dr-die {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    place-items: center;
    width: var(--dr-die-size);
    height: var(--dr-die-size);
    padding: calc(var(--dr-die-size) * 0.12);
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-die-edge);
    border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--dr-die-face);
    transition: border-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .dr-pip {
    width: calc(var(--dr-die-size) * 0.18);
    height: calc(var(--dr-die-size) * 0.18);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--dr-die-pip);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* `nth-of-type`, never `nth-child`. The nine pips are cloned out of an
     `x-for` `<template>`, and that template is itself the die's first element
     child - Alpine inserts the clones as its siblings. So `nth-child` counts the
     template and every face was drawn one pip early and one short: a 3 came out
     as pips 4 and 8 of the grid, a 4 lost two corners, and a 1 and a 6 lost
     pips entirely. `nth-of-type` counts only the `i` elements, so the indices
     below are the pips they read as.

     The indices are the real faces, in reading order across the 3x3:
       1        5        2 = 1,9   3 = 1,5,9   4 = 1,3,7,9   5 = 1,3,5,7,9
                                6 = 1,3,4,6,7,9 */
  .dr-die[data-face='1'] i:nth-of-type(5),
  .dr-die[data-face='2'] i:nth-of-type(1),
  .dr-die[data-face='2'] i:nth-of-type(9),
  .dr-die[data-face='3'] i:nth-of-type(1),
  .dr-die[data-face='3'] i:nth-of-type(5),
  .dr-die[data-face='3'] i:nth-of-type(9),
  .dr-die[data-face='4'] i:nth-of-type(1),
  .dr-die[data-face='4'] i:nth-of-type(3),
  .dr-die[data-face='4'] i:nth-of-type(7),
  .dr-die[data-face='4'] i:nth-of-type(9),
  .dr-die[data-face='5'] i:nth-of-type(1),
  .dr-die[data-face='5'] i:nth-of-type(3),
  .dr-die[data-face='5'] i:nth-of-type(5),
  .dr-die[data-face='5'] i:nth-of-type(7),
  .dr-die[data-face='5'] i:nth-of-type(9),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(1),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(4),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(7),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(3),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(6),
  .dr-die[data-face='6'] i:nth-of-type(9) {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Three states, three shapes of the same box. None of them may set a blanket
     `opacity` on `.dr-pip`: the lit/unlit split is owned by the
     `[data-face]` block above, and a rule here that made every pip faintly
     visible outranked the base `opacity: 0` and painted a full grid of dots on
     every die - a dropped 3 came out as three bright pips on a field of six
     faint ones, which is not a d6 at any size. Each state de-emphasises by
     paint (the face fill and the pip colour), never by alpha.

     `is-socket` is the unrolled state: a blank die face, its nine pips engraved
     rather than lit. A hollow outline read as a checkbox, and four of them read
     as a form; a blank face reads as a die that has not been thrown yet, and it
     is the same object the row will show once the roll lands, so nothing on the
     row changes size or position when the sheet is filled in.

     `is-dropped` no longer goes transparent. It did, when the dice sat on the
     panel, and the only thing that kept a discarded die visible was its dashed
     edge against the surface behind it. Inside the tray, "transparent" means
     the tray's own fill, and a dashed hairline one ramp step off that fill is
     roughly 1.1:1 - invisible. It keeps a fill now, one step off the kept die's,
     and the dashed edge is the boundary; the face is still de-emphasised, which
     1.4.3 exempts as inactive content. */
  .dr-die.is-socket {
    background: var(--dr-die-face);
  }

  .dr-die.is-socket .dr-pip {
    background: var(--dr-die-edge);
    opacity: 0.22;
  }

  .dr-die.is-dropped {
    border-style: dashed;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dr-die-face) 45%, var(--dr-tray));
  }

  .dr-die.is-dropped .dr-pip {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dr-die-pip) 55%, var(--dr-die-face));
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .dr-die.is-dropped:hover {
      border-color: var(--dr-die-edge);
    }
  }

  /* The score plate. Cut rather than raised or inset: a raised plate competes
     with the re-roll for the same hover, and an inset one reads as a hole in
     the sheet. What carries it is the flat step in the neutral ramp and the 1px
     rule - the same treatment as a printed figure on a card. It is 5rem rather
     than 4.5rem because the tray beside it now carries the most ink on the row,
     and the rolled score is the number the player came for: at 4.5rem the plate
     was the same size as the dice it was arguing with. */
  .dr-plate {
    display: flex;
    width: 5rem;
    height: 5rem;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-rule-strong);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--dr-raised);
  }

  /* The Display step, unchanged at 1.75rem. The plate grew from 4.5rem to 5rem
     and the figure did not, deliberately: 2.125rem is not on DESIGN.md's ramp,
     and the score does not need a new step to stay the loudest thing on the
     row - the pips beside it are 0.18 of the die's 1.75rem, so a Display figure
     is already three times the size of anything else in the tray. The bigger
     plate is what stopped it competing with the tray for attention. */
  .dr-sum {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-heading);
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--dr-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* An unrolled plate is a cut slot rather than a raised box. At 5rem it was
     the largest object on an unrolled row and it held a middot, so the arrival
     state was six empty frames - the panel looked like a form waiting for input
     rather than a sheet waiting to be rolled. Dashed, unfilled and with the
     middot in soft ink, it reads as the same blank die face as the sockets
     beside it. */
  .dr-plate.is-empty {
    border-style: dashed;
    border-color: var(--dr-rule);
    background: transparent;
  }

  .dr-sum.is-empty {
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
  }

  /* The modifier, in the theme's action accent: it is the one figure on the
     row that is not a die result but a derived use, and the accent is the
     system's colour for that. */
  .dr-mod {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--dr-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The re-roll, as DESIGN.md's round outline icon button: a hairline rather
     than a fill, because the plate beside it already carries a fill and two
     filled objects on one row is one too many. The ring is bark, never the
     accent it outlines. */
  .dr-reroll {
    display: inline-flex;
    width: 2.75rem;
    height: 2.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-rule-strong);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--dr-accent);
    transition:
      background-color 150ms ease-out,
      transform 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .dr-reroll:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--dr-focus);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .dr-reroll:hover:not(:disabled) {
      background: var(--dr-raised);
    }
  }

  .dr-reroll:active:not(:disabled) {
    transform: translateY(1px);
  }

  .dr-reroll:disabled {
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
    border-color: var(--dr-rule);
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  .dr-glyph {
    transition: transform 200ms ease-out;
  }

  /* The scale is on the button and the rotation on the glyph, so the two
     animate independently and neither needs `transition: all`. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .dr-reroll:hover:not(:disabled) .dr-glyph {
      transform: rotate(180deg);
    }
  }

  /* The rolling indicator. A 2px accent bar at the row's own lower edge, and it
     has to *move*: on `main` this was `animate-pulse`, and the rework replaced it
     with a static bar, which silently dropped a spec requirement (AC 8, "during
     roll, button disabled, animation playing") and, worse, dropped the only
     signal that a row is mid-roll. The re-roll button is disabled for the whole
     300ms, so without motion a press gives no feedback at all until the dice
     land.
     The motion is a sweep of the bar's own width rather than an opacity pulse,
     so it reads as the row filling rather than as the row blinking, and it is
     suppressed under `prefers-reduced-motion` - a decorative loop is exactly
     what that preference is for. */
  .dr-pulse {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: -1px;
    height: 2px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--dr-accent) 22%, transparent);
    z-index: 5;
  }

  .dr-pulse::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    width: 40%;
    background: var(--dr-accent);
    animation: dr-sweep 900ms cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.55, 1) infinite;
  }

  @keyframes dr-sweep {
    from {
      transform: translateX(-100%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(350%);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .dr-pulse::after {
      animation: none;
      width: 100%;
    }
  }

  .dr-foot {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--dr-rule);
  }

  .dr-foot-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* The label, the figure and the scope note on one baseline: the pairing Point
     Buy's foot uses, so the two panels' feet read as the same object and the
     reader has already learned how to parse this one. The scope note is pushed
     to the far end rather than sitting beside the label, because it qualifies the
     figure and a qualifier next to the label would read as a second label. */
  .dr-total-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .dr-total-scope {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }

  /* The figure itself. The Stats step rather than the row scores' Display step,
     because it is one number and the six row scores are already the loudest
     figures on the panel. Heading face, like every other figure on this surface
     that is a number rather than a die. */
  .dr-total {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-heading);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--dr-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .dr-soft {
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
  }

  .dr-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* The system's own atom - an uppercase micro-label over a tabular value - so
     the four figures about one sample read as data and are comparable with the
     figures in the log directly below them. */
  .dr-stat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 3px;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--dr-rule);
    border-radius: 7px;
  }

  .dr-stat-fig {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--dr-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .dr-void {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    max-width: 26rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* The log sits in a well rather than in a second bordered card: a card inside
     a card is two surfaces where one is asked for, and the well is the same
     recessed step the system already uses for a plate. */
  .dr-log {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    max-height: 200px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.625rem;
    border-radius: 7px;
    background: var(--dr-well);
  }

  .dr-log-line {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-table);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--dr-ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }

  /* The newest line takes the panel's own ink, not the action accent. The accent
     measured 3.48:1 on the light well - under the 4.5:1 bar for 12px text, and
     the only reason it was ever there is that it read as emphasis. It is also
     redundant: the foot head already reads "newest first" and `unshift` puts the
     roll you just watched at index 0, so position says which line is current
     and colour only ever had to agree with it. Two steps of the neutral ramp -
     full ink at 600 for the current line, soft ink for the rest - carry the
     same signal at 16.5:1 and 6.7:1 in light, and leave the accent to the
     modifier and the re-roll glyph, which are the only two things on this
     surface that are actually an action or a derived use. */
  .dr-log-line.is-current {
    color: var(--dr-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* The swap panel and its confirm/cancel pair guard the tool's only
     destructive action, so both are painted from the DESIGN.md bark/gold ramp
     rather than the live --sl-color-accent tokens.

     Why not the tokens: --sl-color-accent is #f7860f in dark and #697066 in
     light, and --sl-color-text-invert is --sl-color-accent-low, not white.
     That pairing gives 5.28:1 in dark but only 2.74:1 in light - the light
     theme is the one that fails, which is why text on the accent goes through
     --sl-color-accent-contrast (see #329) and why the swap panel stays off the
     accent entirely. Using the accent for the panel fill is worse still: in
     light it is #b9c0b6, which leaves the gold confirm button at 1.74:1 and
     the cancel button at 1.59:1, i.e. neither keeps a perceivable boundary
     (WCAG 1.4.11).

     Every pairing below is at or above its bar, at rest and on hover:
       confirm fill vs panel  3.00:1 light, 4.67:1 dark   (1.4.11 shape)
       confirm glyph on fill 5.50:1 rest, 4.34:1 hover   (1.4.11, the glyph is
                                                        an SVG graphic and the
                                                        accessible name comes
                                                        from aria-label)
       cancel border/panel  6.68:1 light, 8.42:1 dark    (1.4.11 shape)
       focus ring vs fill   5.50:1 light, 3.00:1 dark    (2.4.11)

     The confirm fill sits exactly on the 3:1 shape boundary against the light
     panel, so there is no headroom to lighten on hover - a lighter gold drops
     the button to 2.26:1 and its edge disappears. The hover therefore darkens to
     #b06f00, which holds every bar in both themes rather than only at rest.
     The ring is never the same value as the button it outlines. */
  .dr-swap {
